      "narrative": "The tavern door swings shut behind you, cutting off the warm copper glow and the low murmur of the Finch. Morning air hits you \u2014 damp, chill, carrying the smell of wet stone and old smoke. The street is narrow, cobbled, slick with overnight rain.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands at the street's end, forty paces ahead. A cracked black arch against a grey-white sky, it rises perhaps twelve feet high, set into a wall that seems older than the buildings around it. No guards. No onlookers. Just the arch, the wall, and the silence that pools around it like water.\n\nAs you approach, details resolve. The arch's stone is veined with soot-dark lines, as if fire once licked its surface. The gate itself \u2014 a slab of black metal, seamless, set flush into the arch \u2014 bears no handle, no hinges visible from this side. Where a lock would sit, there is only a filled channel, the metal filed smooth and flush, the repair work crude but effective. Someone wanted this door to never open again.\n\nAt the base of the arch, a dark stain spreads across the cobbles. Dried, days old. The rain has washed some of it thin, but the shape remains \u2014 a body fell here, and no one bothered to scrub the stone clean.\n\nA single crow perches on the arch's keystone, watching you with one bright eye.\n\nThe Ash Gate waits. Silent. Sealed.\n\nWhat do you do?"
    },
