      "narrative": "You crouch beneath the black arch, running your fingers over the wet cobbles, sweeping aside loose grit and gravel. The bloodstain smears under your touch \u2014 old, tacky, indistinguishable from the grime that has settled into the mortar cracks. You scrape at the base of the sealed metal slab, checking for a dropped key, a hidden latch, a scrap of paper wedged into the seam.\n\nNothing.\n\nYour fingers find only damp stone, cold metal, and the rough edge where the lock was filed away. The cobbles are clean of any loose debris that might have been dropped \u2014 no coins, no torn fabric, no scuff marks that tell a story. If someone stood here before Bram died, the rain has washed every trace away.\n\nThe crow above clicks its beak, a sound almost like a laugh.\n\nYou straighten up, palms damp and dirty, having found precisely nothing. The ground beneath the arch holds no secrets \u2014 at least none you can uncover.\n\nThe street remains empty. The gate remains sealed. The morning cold settles deeper into your bones.\n\nWhat do you do?"
